<!doctype html>
<html lang={assigns[:locale] || "en"}>
  <head>
    <%!-- The member's two crawler opt-outs (search engines via noindex?,
    AI agents/LLMs via noai?) on every page about them. --%>
    <%= if directives = robots_directives(assigns) do %>
      <meta name="robots" content={directives} />
    <% end %>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta http-equiv="X-UA-Compatible" content="IE=edge,chrome=1" />
    <%!-- The hand-off page takes itself onward (VutuvWeb.OutboundHTML): a form
    submission bound for another site has to end at a 200 here, because
    `form-action 'self'` is enforced on a submission's redirects too. Set for
    http(s) destinations only, and by that one response. --%>
    <meta
      :if={assigns[:meta_refresh]}
      http-equiv="refresh"
      content={"0;url=" <> assigns[:meta_refresh]}
    />
    <%!-- live_title so client-side page_title updates (live navigation, tab
    patches) keep the suffix; no suffix on the bare site name. --%>
    <.live_title default="vutuv" suffix={page_title(assigns) && " - vutuv"}>{page_title(assigns)}</.live_title>
    <meta name="description" content={OpenGraph.description(assigns)} />
    <%!-- Link previews (WhatsApp, Facebook, LinkedIn, X, ...):
    VutuvWeb.OpenGraph derives one set of tags per page from the assigns. --%>
    <meta
      :for={{property, content} <- OpenGraph.tags(assigns)}
      property={property}
      content={content}
    />
    <meta name="twitter:card" content={OpenGraph.twitter_card(assigns)} />
    <%!-- Explicit canonical URL, shared one-for-one with og:url
    (VutuvWeb.OpenGraph.canonical_url/1): the request path without volatile
    query params (?lang, ?page), so search engines and AI crawlers index one
    address per page. --%>
    <%= if canonical = OpenGraph.canonical_url(assigns) do %>
      <link rel="canonical" href={canonical} />
    <% end %>
    <%!-- `viewport-fit=cover` lets the page paint edge to edge on a phone with
    a display cutout, which is what makes `env(safe-area-inset-*)` report the
    real insets at all — without it every one of them is 0 and the installed
    app's bottom bar cannot reserve the home indicator's strip (issue #1464).
    What the page then owes the device is its own gutters: `UI.gutter_class/0`
    on the content columns, and the tab bar's own padding in ShellLive. --%>
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover" />
    <meta name="csrf-token" content={get_csrf_token()} />
    <%!-- Installed web app (issue #1464). The manifest is what tells iOS which
    links belong to the app: anything outside its `scope` opens in an in-app
    Safari overlay on top of the app, and with no manifest at all that is what
    a member got for ordinary navigation. The two `*-capable` metas are the
    pre-manifest spelling of the same wish, kept for iOS before 17.4 and for
    Chrome's legacy path; a browser that reads the manifest ignores them. --%>
    <link rel="manifest" href={~p"/site.webmanifest"} />
    <meta name="mobile-web-app-capable" content="yes" />
    <meta name="apple-mobile-web-app-capable" content="yes" />
    <%!-- Site icons. iMessage's LinkPresentation renders the page and then looks for
    an icon; when every candidate 404s it drops the rich link preview and shows the
    bare URL, so these files must exist (test/vutuv_web/site_icons_test.exs). --%>
    <link rel="icon" href="/favicon.ico" sizes="32x32" />
    <link rel="icon" href="/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml" />
    <link rel="apple-touch-icon" href="/apple-touch-icon.png" />
    <meta name="theme-color" content="#ffffff" media="(prefers-color-scheme: light)" />
    <meta name="theme-color" content="#020617" media="(prefers-color-scheme: dark)" />
    <%!-- Agent-format discovery (VutuvWeb.AgentDocs): pages that exist as
    Markdown / text / JSON (/ vCard) advertise the sibling URLs here. --%>
    <link
      :for={alt <- assigns[:agent_doc_alternates] || []}
      rel="alternate"
      type={alt.type}
      href={alt.href}
      title={alt[:title]}
    />
    <%!-- rel="me" identity links (profile pages): the member's listed social
    accounts, read by Mastodon's link verification (VutuvWeb.UserController). --%>
    <link :for={me <- assigns[:rel_me_urls] || []} rel="me" href={me} />
    <%!-- Federating members advertise their ActivityPub actor document. --%>
    <link
      :if={assigns[:activity_json_alternate]}
      rel="alternate"
      type="application/activity+json"
      href={assigns[:activity_json_alternate]}
    />
    <%!-- `phx-track-static` is how a LiveView finds out that the browser it is
    patching still runs the *previous* release's bundle. A deploy does not
    reload an open page: the socket reconnects to the new release and patches
    its markup into a document whose stylesheet and hooks are whatever the
    browser downloaded hours ago. The client reports these two hrefs on join
    and `static_changed?/1` compares them against the digest manifest, which is
    what lets a page skip markup that would arrive unstyled and unhooked (see
    the feed's tab ticker). Without the annotation the client sends nothing and
    every such check answers "unchanged". --%>
    <link phx-track-static rel="stylesheet" href={~p"/assets/app.css"} />
    <script defer phx-track-static src={~p"/assets/app.js"}></script>
  </head>
  <body class={assigns[:conn] && @conn.assigns[:body_class]}>
    {@inner_content}
  </body>
</html>
